ZTEXT001000017acb70a27c10000000
ZERO BROWSER READER 0.13

This original Zero module is a bounded text reader for HTML and plain text. It
does not include Chromium code or another browser engine. External HTML is input
for interoperability; the reader produces our own ZBROW001 document format.

Current operation

The reader accepts at most 65536 input bytes. The complete input must be strict
UTF-8: overlong sequences, surrogate encodings, values above U+10FFFF and truncated
sequences fail before parsing. HTML tags are compared without ASCII case.
The engine recognizes headings, paragraphs, divisions, line breaks, lists,
preformatted text, block containers, simple table rows/cells, title and links.
Comments and script/style contents are suppressed. The head is hidden except
for its title. Script code, forms, styles and media are never executed.

Normal text whitespace is collapsed. Preformatted text preserves spaces and
line breaks; tabs become a single space. C0 and C1 control characters are
sanitized. The text hard-wraps at the requested number of Unicode scalar values
per line. This is not Unicode shaping, word wrapping, display-width calculation,
or a guarantee that the operating-system font contains those glyphs.

Entities supported are lt, gt, amp, quot, apos and nbsp with a semicolon, plus
decimal and hexadecimal numeric references. Numeric zero and surrogates become
U+FFFD. Unknown or invalid references are kept literally and flagged.

Anchors accept quoted and unquoted href values and ASCII-insensitive attribute
names. The first href attribute wins. Ampersand query separators written as
amp entities are decoded. An empty, control-containing, whitespace-containing
or overlong href is not added. The maximum is 32 links, numbered from 1.
Labels and title are limited to 127 bytes without writing partial UTF-8 scalars;
href is limited to 255 bytes. Active links receive a visible [N] marker.
URL resolution and allowed schemes belong to the caller, not this parser.

Command line

zero-browser-reader reads HTML from standard input and writes one binary
ZBROW001 document to standard output. --plain keeps literal angle brackets,
ampersands and text line breaks, with the same UTF-8 and size checks. The default
wrapper width is 78. No diagnostics are mixed into the binary output.
Exit 64 means an unknown argument; 65 means invalid input; 28 means text output
capacity exceeded; 74 means a read/write failure. Errors write no document.

Memory API

html-declarations.zero.part plus html.zero.part define the memory-only reader.
Inputs: h_input, h_length, h_output, h_capacity, h_width, h_title, h_links,
h_scratch and h_plain (0 for HTML, nonzero for plain text).
Outputs: h_written, h_linkcount, h_error and h_flags.
The caller owns valid, disjoint buffers and may invoke the module repeatedly.
Input length is 0..65536; output capacity is 1..32768 including its final NUL;
width is 20..160. Title is 128 bytes, links are 16384 bytes, scratch is 512 bytes.
The input is never modified. On error counts are reset and text/title start
with NUL. Any partial link or scratch content must be discarded on error.
This is a memory-safe bounded parser within the caller's valid-buffer contract,
not a general pointer-validation or sandbox mechanism.

ZBROW001 document

All integer fields are unsigned little-endian 64-bit values.
0: eight ASCII bytes ZBROW001
8: version 1
16: text byte length, at most 32767
24: link count, at most 32
32: flags
40: error, always 0 in a serialized successful document
48: width, currently 78
56: reserved zero
64: title, 128 bytes, NUL terminated
192: 32 link records, 512 bytes each
16576: UTF-8 text bytes
The serialized length is exactly 16576 plus text length. The final text NUL is
private working memory and is not serialized. A caller using string APIs must
provide one additional byte and append its own NUL after validation.
Each link record has href at 0 for 256 bytes; label at 256 for 128 bytes;
label byte length at 384; remaining bytes reserved zero. Unused records are zero.

Flags

1: script encountered
2: style encountered
4: form/control encountered
8: more than 32 links encountered
16: unsupported image/media/embed encountered
32: a title, label or href exceeded its limit or a href was rejected
64: an unknown or invalid entity was retained
128: preformatted HTML encountered

Limits and unsupported behavior

This profile is not HTML5 error recovery or a DOM/CSS implementation. Incomplete
tags and quoted attributes fail. An unterminated comment, script or style hides
the remaining corresponding content. Malformed nesting receives bounded simple
handling rather than the browser-standard tree-building algorithm.
There is no JavaScript, CSS layout, forms submission, cookies, storage, fonts,
image decoder, audio/video, worker API, accessibility tree, WebGL or WebAssembly.
Network HTTPS/TLS capability is separate from this reader and must not be
inferred from its ability to parse an external document.

Composition and tests

reader.zero is composed in this order: reader-head.zero.part,
html-declarations.zero.part, reader-input.zero.part, html.zero.part,
reader-output.zero.part. html-check.zero uses html-check-head.zero.part,
html-declarations.zero.part, html-check-input.zero.part, html.zero.part and
html-check-output.zero.part. Both are compiled by our existing Zero compiler.

The original Zero regression suite executes 70 semantic/boundary cases and 100
deterministic malformed ASCII probes. Every case checks guards before and after
each buffer, unchanged input checksum, valid output length and NUL termination.
The suite covers UTF-8 rejection/preservation, entities, raw script text, quoted
tag delimiters, boolean attributes, field boundaries, exact output capacities,
64 KiB input, 32/33 links, head/title, links, lists, pre and --plain behavior.
Two sentinel-pointer cases verify address-span overflow cannot reach cleanup.
Linux native execution passes. Linux native, Windows x64, guest x64 and guest
x86 compilation succeeds. Windows compilation is not a Windows execution test.
